
  [1mA. Plain-script global[0m — new [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m with a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing
  [38;5;153mwindow.Tracking.getUserId()[39m. Matches exactly how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m works today; zero new concepts, zero config. Cost: a global,
  and it can't be imported by anything in [38;5;153msrc/[39m or tested in Node without a shim. Wins if you want the smallest possible
  footprint and don't expect this to grow.

  [1mB. Native ES module[0m [3m(recommended)[0m — [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m exports [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with [38;5;153m<script [39m
  [38;5;153mtype="module">[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports from it. No globals, no build step (native ESM is fine in every current browser),
  and Node can import the same file directly so it's testable without tooling gymnastics. Cost: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes a module,
  which changes its load semantics (deferred, its functions stop being globals — nothing currently depends on that, I
  checked), and it means deciding the repo's module story rather than dodging it. Moderate.

  [1mC. Dual CommonJS/global wrapper[0m — one file that detects [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m and otherwise attaches to [38;5;153mwindow[39m. Testable in
  Node today, browser stays a plain script tag. Cost: boilerplate wrapper around every shared file, and it entrenches
  CommonJS in a browser app. Wins only if you specifically want Node tests without touching [38;5;153mindex.html[39m's script tags.

  [1mMy recommendation: B.[0m You told me other forms will consume this, which makes it shared infrastructure rather than a
  one-off — and shared infrastructure behind a global (A) gets painful about the third consumer, while C pays wrapper
  boilerplate forever to avoid a one-line change in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. B is the only one that leaves you with a real module
  boundary and a straightforward testing story, and the conversion cost is genuinely small at this size. If you'd rather
  not touch [38;5;153mindex.html[39m's loading at all, A is the honest second choice and I won't pretend it's wrong for a stub this
  small.
